.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 102 PM PDT Thu Oct 22 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

There remains a slight chance of thunderstorms across mainly the
Central Coast between now and around 5 PM to 7 PM this afternoon.
This is as up to 500 J/kg of Surface Based CAPE is forecast across
this region, with areas farther to the north likely to remain
rain showers at there has been a lack of daytime warming occuring
due to mid/high level clouds. There are breaks in the sky cover
over the north Central Coast especially over Monterey and San
Benito counties. Rain showers will linger into late this evening
across the Santa Lucia range and the Mountains of San Benito and
Interior Monterey County.

Drier conditions return for Friday as weak high pressure builds
in the wake of the exiting trough. Additionally, widespread
sunshine is expected by Friday afternoon allowing for additional
warming. However, maximum temperatures Friday are forecast to be
near normal for the North Bay to up to 10 degrees below across the
Central Coast.

Periods of moderate northerly winds are likely across the North
Bay Interior Mountains Friday night into Saturday morning. Winds
across this region have the potential to be between 15-25 mph with
gusts up to 45 mph, potentially stronger in the highest peaks.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 102 PM PDT Thu Oct 22 2026
(Saturday through next Wednesday)

A gradual warming and drying trend will persist across the region
as high pressure continues to build in from the eastern Pacific.
However, through the weekend temperatures only return to near
seasonal averages and look to continue that way through much of
next week.

The more significant threat will be around low-lying areas along
the San Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and along the coast are
likely to see minor coastal flooding over the weekend during peak
high tides. This is likely to persist into at least the middle of
the upcoming week.

